00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:58.299
Our next step is to do stoichiometry.

00:05:58.759 --> 00:06:04.129
When it comes down to doing solution
stoichiometry, we need to be

00:06:04.129 --> 00:06:06.299
able to map out our pathway here.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:12.909
When it comes to solutions, the idea is,
is that we need to start with a volume of

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:15.439
one ingredient and convert it to moles.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:17.890
moles are always the goal.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.950
So how do we do that?

00:06:20.969 --> 00:06:25.099
Well, the only way to really be
able to bridge from volume to

00:06:25.099 --> 00:06:29.220
moles is by using molarity, right?

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:33.549
Molarity is a conversion term
that is very useful to us.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.684
So that is bridged by molarity of.

00:06:37.294 --> 00:06:39.655
In this case, molarity of
A to get the moles of A.

00:06:40.505 --> 00:06:44.854
So what that tells you is the only
place that you can start is with

00:06:44.914 --> 00:06:47.625
a chemical that has That's a both.

00:06:49.445 --> 00:06:51.425
of both the molarity and the volume.

00:06:51.445 --> 00:06:53.395
You need both things to start.

00:06:53.405 --> 00:06:58.815
Both molarity, volume and
molarity is where you start.

00:07:01.735 --> 00:07:09.965
So when we do that, we can then convert it
to moles of B, which we can then convert

00:07:11.265 --> 00:07:15.555
to volume of B using the other molarity.

00:07:15.985 --> 00:07:20.725
So typically you're given one, maybe
potentially two concentrations here.

00:07:21.135 --> 00:07:24.735
So we'll use the other molarity
to do that, which was our HNO3.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.460
So the thing we're going to
start with is barium hydroxide.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:29.560
So that is where we will start.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:33.220
So why don't we copy our
information from up here.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:41.049
Uh, so we'll have our chemical equation
with us and then our variables.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:43.270
All right.

00:07:44.489 --> 00:07:45.900
So let's bring that down.

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:47.590
All right.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.880
So we have our problem here.

00:07:50.219 --> 00:07:53.150
What we're going to do is we're
going to set up a stoichiometry

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:54.880
process for ourselves.

00:07:55.550 --> 00:08:01.370
So we'll first have our volume is
what we said we would start with.

00:08:02.039 --> 00:08:03.880
So we'll have 27.

00:08:03.939 --> 00:08:05.619
2 milliliters.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:08.329
Now our goal is to convert that to moles.

00:08:08.809 --> 00:08:10.490
So what is molarity?

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:11.780
Let's take a look.

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:13.840
Molarity is moles.

00:08:14.419 --> 00:08:15.770
Over liters.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:20.409
So if I have my barium hydroxide,
that is saying that we have

00:08:20.409 --> 00:08:25.330
0.245 moles over liters.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:29.040
Now, is there another way that this can be
represented to make this a little easier?

00:08:29.045 --> 00:08:30.030
Well, there is.

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:34.549
So if you think about being that this
problem has to be in milliliters,

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:36.640
how many milliliters are in a liter?

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:40.710
Well, you might remember 1000
milliliters is one liter.

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:44.260
So we can actually
represent this two ways.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:45.640
We can just say 0.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:51.329
245 moles is over 1, 000
milliliters or over 1 liter.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:52.169
It's a shortcut.

00:08:52.629 --> 00:08:55.590
So it's always good to have those
little shortcuts for ourselves.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:57.400
So let's plug that in.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:00.790
So if we have that 0.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:09.510
245 moles of barium hydroxide, and
that's over 1, 000 milliliters, Now,

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:14.170
why is that important is the MLs
now cancel the MLs, they're gone,

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:18.150
and all we have is moles of barium
hydroxide, which was our first step.

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:22.539
Now, why did we have to balance
the chemical equation earlier?

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:25.780
Well, that was so that we can
know the mole to mole ratio.

00:09:26.649 --> 00:09:33.070
So, our next step is that we'll have
one mole of barium hydroxide, And

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:39.719
that's equivalent to 2 moles of nitric
acid based on our chemical equation.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:44.040
So that cancels moles of barium hydroxide.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:45.800
That goes away.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:48.940
Now we have to convert it to volume.

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:56.239
And we'll do the same trick with the
molarity of nitric acid, which is 0.

00:09:56.239 --> 00:09:57.050
450 moles.

00:09:57.555 --> 00:10:03.285
Over one liter or over 1000
milliliters being that our final

00:10:03.334 --> 00:10:05.295
answer has to be in milliliters.

00:10:05.944 --> 00:10:06.934
So let's plug that in.

00:10:07.385 --> 00:10:11.664
So we need to set it up so units
cancel, they have to be opposites.

00:10:14.295 --> 00:10:17.564
And this is going to
give us our final volume.

00:10:17.814 --> 00:10:21.305
And if you have to put it in liters
for you, maybe for your homework that

00:10:21.305 --> 00:10:26.165
you have for your class, then you can
you can put you can leave it in over

00:10:26.165 --> 00:10:28.104
one liter if that helps you as well.

00:10:28.105 --> 00:10:28.229
Bye bye.

00:10:28.589 --> 00:10:32.420
You have either option, but you can
see all those units are canceling

00:10:32.459 --> 00:10:36.299
out as we go through this process and
let's pick a different color here.

00:10:36.299 --> 00:10:40.780
Just so You can help illustrate
the differences here of

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:42.010
all these things canceling.

00:10:42.939 --> 00:10:43.329
All right.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:45.480
Now, when we solve, what are we doing?

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:48.629
We're multiplying everything on
top, dividing what's on bottom.

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.209
So we have 27.

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:52.080
2 milliliters times 0.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:55.750
245 moles times two
moles times a thousand.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:59.429
We'll then divide that by a
thousand, divide that by 0.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:04.240
450 and what we are going to get is 29.

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:06.260
62 milliliters.

00:11:06.875 --> 00:11:10.974
of nitric acid is required
to neutralize this.

00:11:11.574 --> 00:11:16.915
Now we want to make sure we put in the
right sig figs, so this is going to be 29.

00:11:16.915 --> 00:11:21.354
6 mL is going to be our
final answer of nitric acid.

00:11:22.164 --> 00:11:26.015
And that is how we do
this style of problem.

00:11:26.955 --> 00:11:27.954
Let's go into the next one.

00:11:29.685 --> 00:11:35.025
So if I had the following example,
which says calculate the pH of a strong

00:11:35.045 --> 00:11:42.005
base solution If you use 5 grams of
calcium hydroxide to make a total of 2.

00:11:42.275 --> 00:11:46.074
5 liters of solution, what is the pH?

00:11:47.354 --> 00:11:48.785
How do we use a pH equation?

00:11:48.825 --> 00:11:52.494
Well, one of the big things
about any of those is that it

00:11:52.494 --> 00:11:56.485
comes down to identifying, is
this an acid or is this a base?

00:11:56.895 --> 00:12:00.685
So the ingredient that we were given
in the very beginning was calcium

00:12:00.685 --> 00:12:06.584
hydroxide, which if you remember, anything
that has a hydroxide in it is a base.

00:12:06.585 --> 00:12:06.659
Thanks.

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:12.740
So this one is going to be, we
can only solve for POH first.

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:17.860
So let's write how this is going to
dissociate because we want to know how

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:20.420
much hydroxide there is in solution.

00:12:21.209 --> 00:12:27.809
So if this breaks apart, we have
one calcium ion and two hydroxide

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:30.920
ions that are in solution, right?

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:33.730
So both of these are
in their aqueous state.

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:37.959
And they are dissolving well now why?

00:12:37.989 --> 00:12:39.099
Why is that important?

00:12:39.129 --> 00:12:44.430
Well, we were only given how many
grams of calcium hydroxide we have to

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:47.189
convert that to moles of hydroxide.

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:48.780
Why moles?

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:55.369
Well, remember our formula is POH
is equal to the negative log of the

00:12:55.369 --> 00:13:01.485
concentration of oh minus, meaning
that oh minus has to be in molarity.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:04.880
So we have to go from moles
and then divide it by the

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:06.440
liters that are in solution.

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:10.140
So we have to use, uh, a few
different pieces of information

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:10.819
that are here, right?

00:13:10.819 --> 00:13:11.139
We have the 2.

00:13:11.349 --> 00:13:13.340
5 liters and the 0.

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:15.369
5 grams that are here.

00:13:15.899 --> 00:13:17.900
So let's first calculate moles.

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:18.899
Let's go from 0.

00:13:18.899 --> 00:13:24.469
5 grams of calcium hydroxide
to moles of hydroxide.

00:13:25.009 --> 00:13:28.310
So when we are doing that, we're
going to map out our reaction, right?

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:31.400
So we're going to go from grams to moles.

00:13:31.844 --> 00:13:38.545
And that's for calcium hydroxide and then
we are going to go to moles again using,

00:13:39.035 --> 00:13:47.275
uh, that one mole of calcium is equivalent
to two moles to two moles of hydroxide.

00:13:47.645 --> 00:13:48.045
Okay.

00:13:48.454 --> 00:13:50.444
So map out your process.

00:13:50.555 --> 00:13:54.634
It makes it a little bit easier
when you're trying to figure out

00:13:54.685 --> 00:13:59.944
exactly what is it that you are
trying to work on in your problem.

00:14:00.635 --> 00:14:04.275
So, With that, we will
determine the concentration.

00:14:04.694 --> 00:14:07.245
So we started with 0.

00:14:07.485 --> 00:14:11.035
5 grams of calcium hydroxide.

00:14:12.175 --> 00:14:15.505
We need to multiply
that by the molar mass.

00:14:16.995 --> 00:14:21.825
So the molar mass, when you look up
and calculate those values, it's 73.

00:14:24.345 --> 00:14:31.464
968 grams of calcium hydroxide
per mole of calcium hydroxide.

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:36.780
So when we do that, we'll
see the grams cancels grams.

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:42.750
And now the next step is we have to
do the mole to mole equation, which

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:49.980
as we saw earlier is that one mole
of calcium hydroxide dissociated

00:14:50.039 --> 00:14:53.320
into two moles of hydroxide.

00:14:54.339 --> 00:14:58.150
So now when we do that, we can see
that, all right, the moles cancel and it

00:14:58.150 --> 00:15:00.300
gives us how much hydroxide is in there.

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.300
Now remember, this is
not what the molarity is.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:05.310
This is only how many moles there are.

00:15:06.014 --> 00:15:10.314
So, when we solve that, we'll have 0.

00:15:10.315 --> 00:15:13.155
0135 moles of hydroxide.

00:15:13.944 --> 00:15:15.535
Now how do you find a concentration?

00:15:16.004 --> 00:15:20.095
Well, this is where we have to treat
molarity like a formula, right?

00:15:20.105 --> 00:15:23.084
Molarity is moles over liters.

00:15:23.625 --> 00:15:28.075
So we're going to plug in
our moles and our liters.

00:15:28.075 --> 00:15:29.905
So we'll put moles on top.

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:35.670
And then we'll divide it by how
many liters of solution are,

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:39.480
which were 2.5 liters of solution.

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:46.660
Now, when we do that, we will get our
final molarity, which is 5.4 times 10 to

00:15:46.665 --> 00:15:51.760
the negative three molar of hydroxide.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:52.810
Okay?

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:58.210
So that's the oh minus concentration, and
that's our important value there, right?

